// kalman2d_predict.eml -- N-D Kalman covariance propagation (predict step).
//
//   P' = F * P * transpose(F) + Q
//
// The MATRIX generalization of kalman_1d's scalar variance drift
// (`p + q`): a full covariance matrix pushed forward through the state-
// transition F and inflated by the process-noise covariance Q. This is
// the predict half of every multi-state Kalman filter -- a constant-
// velocity tracker (position + velocity), an IMU attitude estimator, a
// battery state-of-charge + internal-resistance model.
//
// eml-stdlib's first MATRIX-OPERATOR kernel. The body is written the way
// the math is written -- `F * P * transpose(F) + Q` -- and the compiler
// lowers the operators (matmul, transpose, matrix-add) to the flat
// per-element form every backend already emits. One source becomes C /
// Rust / C++ / Java / ... AND a machine-checked Lean theorem, and is
// regression-gated to ULP across every target with a real toolchain
// (tests/equivalence/test_matrix_cross_target.py).
//
// The @verify contract is the CENTRAL structural invariant of a covariance
// matrix: SYMMETRY is preserved. A covariance is symmetric by definition,
// and the congruence-plus-noise F*P*Fᵀ + Q keeps it symmetric -- if the
// prior P and the process noise Q are symmetric, so is P'. Proven
// sorryAx-free (mach_ring, no classical axioms) as the cross-element
// equation result[0][1] = result[1][0], given the symmetric-input
// preconditions. A filter that silently breaks covariance symmetry
// diverges (the gain computation assumes it); this contract guarantees the
// predict step never does.
//
// (Entrywise non-negativity is ALSO provable per element via mach_sign;
// the remaining structural property -- positive-semidefiniteness of P' --
// needs quadratic-form / semidefinite reasoning beyond ring identities and
// is the next frontier. The numeric cross-target-equivalence gate pins the
// arithmetic itself to ULP across every target.)

module kalman2d_predict;

@verify(lean, theorem = "kalman2d_cov_predict_symmetric")
fn cov_predict(F: Mat<2,2>, P: Mat<2,2>, Q: Mat<2,2>) -> Mat<2,2>
    where chain_order <= 0
    requires (P[0][1] == P[1][0])
    requires (Q[0][1] == Q[1][0])
    ensures (result[0][1] == result[1][0])
{
    F * P * transpose(F) + Q
}
